// example_macro_group_arity/main.mir — REJECTION CASE, pinning a diagnostic that only became
// accurate once the two call-resolution trees were unified (CHECK-7).
//
// A macro yields exactly one value, so binding two names to it is an arity error. But
// check_group_call_returns had no MacroSymbol arm in its symbol dispatch — check_expr's
// otherwise-identical tree did — so a macro in group position fell through to the catch-all
// and reported "'twice' is not callable", which is false: it is callable, just not into two
// slots. The cascade ("unknown identifier 'a'") followed from that.
//
// This is the class of divergence the finding was about: the two trees were ~90% identical,
// and the 10% was not a deliberate difference, it was a gap.
//
// Expected: rejected with "group declaration expects 2 return value(s), got 1".

macro twice(n: i32) -> n * 2

pub fn main() -> i32 {
    const a, b := twice(5)
    return a + b
}
